## 20260821

0.13.0: Update the two vendored libraries which had drifted, and drop the twenty-two files nothing reaches.

1. ~ lib/File/self.grep.rb: 0.0.0 --> 0.0.1; lib/FileUtils/where.rb: 0.0.3 --> 0.1.0. Taken from ~/lib/ruby by vendor_requires --overwrite, the repository copy being the older in both cases.
2. self.grep 0.0.1 adds the require of Regexp/to_regexp which 0.0.0 was missing. In isolation the old copy raises NoMethodError on File.grep, which status() calls in both its branches, but nothing was broken: MacOS/IfConfig pulls Regexp/to_regexp in first, so by the time status() runs the method is there. Checked against the script's whole require set rather than the one file.
3. - Twenty-two files from lib, nothing reaching them once where.rb was updated: it no longer requires Platform/OS or File/self.basename_without_extname, so Files.rb, Ordinal, Version.rb, the Platform tree and the Array and Module extensions behind them fall away with it. Thirty-five files to thirteen.
4. Files.rb was what required ostruct, which audit_requires had reported as answered only by an undeclared gem. That require is gone with it. The formula still wants its ostruct resource, switches.rb declaring ostruct as a dependency of its own.
5. Checked rather than assumed: audit_requires reports nothing missing against the thirteen, and each call the script makes was run against the trimmed lib — MacOS::VPN.up?, IfConfig.up_interfaces, IfConfig.destroy_interface, FileUtils.which, File.grep, Sys::ProcTable.ps and run() with show:.
6. IfConfig here was already 0.7.0, and run.rb already 0.3.0, so neither carried the fault startor's 0.6.0 IfConfig did.


## 20260821

0.12.1: Fetch Homebrew's installer from where it now lives.

1. ~ setup(): .../install/master/install --> .../install/HEAD/install.sh. The old URL answers 404, so curl -fsSL fetched nothing, bash ran an empty script, and the `brew install wireguard-tools` after it failed for want of brew. The interpreter was already right here, this being bash rather than the ruby startor had.
2. It went unnoticed because setup() only reaches that line where brew is absent, which it has not been on any machine this has run on.
3. The form is the one homebrew-tap's own README gives, which is Homebrew's published line. startor carried the same dead URL and is fixed at its 0.9.1.


## 20260821

0.12.0: + README.md, including what was in the headers.

1. + README.md. Description, Installation, Dependencies, Usage, Notes and Contributing, following the shape of the READMEs the other programs in the tap carry.
2. The Description says what the name does not: this is a WireGuard endpoint switcher and has nothing to do with the version control system, which is the first thing anyone reading the tap's list would wonder.
3. The Usage section covers the five commands and the four switches, the --config resolution among them: a path is taken as given, a name is looked for in ~/.config/wireguard, and anything else is matched against the configuration names case insensitively.
4. The Notes record the sudoers entries wanted for unattended use, the state kept in /tmp/mercurial-*.tmp, the lock and its five minute staleness, and why a switch clears away stuck processes and interfaces before it starts.
5. - The Examples and Notes sections from the script header. The README holds that record now, and keeping both invites them to disagree, which they had: Examples item 3 said `mercurial` is the same as `mercurial switch`, and that stopped being so at 0.9.0, whose item 15 reads "~ main(): Add 'up' command alias and usage output for no/unknown command". It went three versions saying otherwise, which is the argument for one record rather than two.
6. Ten of the twelve examples are in the README's Usage, `mercurial setup` being in its Installation and the bare invocation its Note 5. The sudoers entries the Notes carried are its Note 2, written against your_username rather than mine.
7. Examples 7 and 8 gave `--config /full/path/to/config.conf` and `--config ~/config.conf`, which switch() does not honour: a config matching /\// is checked for existence and then reduced by File.basename, and up() joins that name to the configs path. A configuration kept elsewhere is never opened. The README says what happens rather than what was intended, and the code is left alone, that being a behaviour to settle rather than to document away.
8. The README's Usage mirrors the script's own usage(), both the five commands and the switches, with the short forms the Examples never gave: -d, -c, -r and -f. --format is not among the options usage() prints, though :format has been in switches() since 0.10.0, which is where the JSON output came from.
9. Where the only configuration in ~/.config/wireguard is the one already up, random_wireguard_config() draws from an empty list and returns nil, and File.join raises TypeError. The README says to keep more than one. The behaviour is left alone.
10. The Notes the header carried gave two NOPASSWD entries, for wg-quick and /bin/kill, and called that enough for automated execution. A run reaches sudo six ways, not two: wg-quick up and down, rm -f over /var/run/wireguard and pkill for wireguard-go in the cleanup, kill -9 for a stuck wg-quick, and ifconfig destroy where the interface survives the down. The README gives five entries and names all six calls.
11. What the header documented was a subset of what this machine has had configured since May: /etc/sudoers.d/thoran already carries the rm, pkill and ifconfig entries, which is why the omission never showed. The record was incomplete rather than the setup.
12. The wg-quick path in those entries is Homebrew's on Apple silicon. The script takes whatever FileUtils.which finds, so the README says to match `command -v wg-quick` rather than to copy the path.
13. ~ Note 1, which had the interfaces handled through ifconfig and networksetup. networksetup is startor's, for the SOCKS proxy, and appears nowhere in this repository.


## 20260816

0.11.2: Populate .gitignore, which had been committed empty.

1. ~ .gitignore: 47 lines, covering gem builds, bundler, RDoc and YARD output, .DS_Store, editor files, the Ruby version managers, test artefacts and .claude. The file existed and was empty, which git-boot creates it as and nothing had since filled in.


## 20260815

0.11.1: Vendor Regexp/to_regexp.rb, which was resolving from RUBYLIB rather than from the repository.

1. + lib/Regexp/to_regexp.rb. MacOS/IfConfig requires it and it had never been vendored, so it resolved here only because RUBYLIB supplies it and nowhere else. A Homebrew install, which has no RUBYLIB of its own, is what exposed it: the installed mercurial could not load at all.
2. vendor_requires named this file at the start of the tap work and it went unacted upon until the formula was converted and the program run. Reporting it is not the same as fixing it.


